In the code above, the producer should be closed if any of the following steps fail. The other doBuild methods have similar issues.

Custom task assignors may use different representations for a subtopology with no assigned tasks. Some implementations omit the subtopology entry, while others include it with an empty task set:
 
{code:java}
// No standby tasks: omit the subtopology entry.
Map.of()

// No standby tasks: include the subtopology with an empty set.
Map.of("subtopology-1", Set.of()){code}
Both representations assign zero standby tasks. The public MemberAssignment API does not explicitly require empty subtopology entries to be omitted, and the coordinator accepts and persists them.

However, the target assignment preserves empty entries, while reconciliation omits them from the current assignment. TasksTuple.sameTasks() treats these representations as different. As a result, an otherwise stable Streams group can trigger an unnecessary assignment refinement on every subsequent heartbeat, even though its actual task assignment has not changed.

This advances the group, target assignment, and member epochs and generates additional coordinator records. Reconciliation leaves the same representation difference in place, so the next heartbeat triggers another update.

Here, an unnecessary rebalance means an epoch and assignment update in the coordinator. In the reproduction, the assignor was called only once; the subsequent updates did not require rerunning it. Task restarts or data loss have not been demonstrated.
